---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ifrs/ifrs10.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Control Model" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Power Over the Investee" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Variable Returns" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Linkage of Power & Returns" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Investment Entities" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Loss of Control" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Consolidation Procedures" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IFRS vs US GAAP" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
        { id: "history", label: "History & Development" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why a SINGLE control model was needed</div>
        <div class="principle">The Conceptual Framework defines a reporting entity's boundary by what it genuinely CONTROLS. Before IFRS 10, control assessments differed across standards and were often based purely on VOTING RIGHTS percentages — allowing entities to structure arrangements (e.g. "structured entities" with minimal voting rights but genuine economic control) to avoid consolidation. IFRS 10 introduced a SINGLE, substance-based control model applicable to ALL types of investees.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IFRS 10 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong>Substance over form</strong> — control is assessed based on POWER and RETURNS, not merely legal voting percentages, capturing arrangements that gave genuine control WITHOUT majority voting rights</span>
          <span><strong>Faithful representation</strong> — consolidating only what is GENUINELY controlled ensures the group accounts represent the true economic entity, avoiding both under- and over-consolidation</span>
          <span><strong>Comparability</strong> — a SINGLE control model across all investee types (subsidiaries, structured entities, etc.) replaced multiple different consolidation triggers previously scattered across IAS 27 and SIC interpretations</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IFRS 10 was issued DIRECTLY in response to the 2008 financial crisis, where numerous "off-balance-sheet" special purpose/structured entities were revealed to be effectively controlled by their sponsors despite minimal formal voting rights — a major consolidation loophole IFRS 10 was designed to close.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The three-element control test, at a glance</div>
        <div class="principle">An investor controls an investee when it is exposed to variable returns AND has the ability to affect those returns through power.</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Control = Power over the investee + Exposure (rights) to variable returns + Ability to USE power to affect the amount of returns</div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--chip callout--flush">ℹ️ ALL THREE elements must be present SIMULTANEOUSLY — missing any one means control (and hence consolidation) does NOT exist.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IFRS 10 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRS 10 establishes principles for the presentation and preparation of consolidated financial statements when an entity controls one or more OTHER entities. A PARENT (an entity controlling one or more subsidiaries) presents consolidated financial statements, with LIMITED exceptions (e.g. certain intermediate parents meeting specific conditions, or investment entities per Section 7).</div>
        <div class="note">IFRS 10's control model applies UNIVERSALLY — to traditional operating subsidiaries AND to "structured entities" (special purpose vehicles) where voting rights are not the dominant factor.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Control of an investee</div><div class="glossary-def">An investor controls an investee when it is exposed, or has rights, to variable returns from its involvement, and has the ability to affect those returns through power over the investee.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Power</div><div class="glossary-def">Existing rights that give the current ability to direct the RELEVANT ACTIVITIES — the activities that significantly affect the investee's returns.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Structured entity</div><div class="glossary-def">An entity designed so that voting/similar rights are NOT the dominant factor in deciding who controls it (e.g. voting rights relate only to administrative tasks, with relevant activities directed by contractual arrangements).</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Control Model — Three Elements Working Together</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Reassessment is continuous, not one-off</div>
        <div class="principle">An investor REASSESSES whether it controls an investee if facts and circumstances indicate CHANGES to one or more of the three control elements — control is NOT a static, one-time assessment made only at initial investment.</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong>Power</strong> — over the RELEVANT ACTIVITIES (Section 4)</span>
          <span><strong>Returns</strong> — exposure or rights to VARIABLE returns from involvement (Section 5)</span>
          <span><strong>Linkage</strong> — the ability to USE power to AFFECT the amount of those returns (Section 6)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This continuous reassessment requirement means a change in an investee's governance structure, contractual arrangements, or even market conditions can trigger a NEW control conclusion mid-relationship, without any new investment being made.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Power Over the Investee</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Beyond simple majority voting</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Power arises from RIGHTS — which can include voting rights, rights to appoint/remove key management, rights to direct transactions for the investor's benefit, or OTHER contractual arrangements. POTENTIAL voting rights (e.g. convertible instruments, options) are considered ONLY if SUBSTANTIVE — the holder must have the PRACTICAL ABILITY to exercise them.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Worked example — de facto control without majority voting</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Control with less than 50% voting rights</div>
          <div class="wk-body">An investor holds 45% of an investee's voting rights. The remaining 55% is held by thousands of small, widely dispersed shareholders, none individually holding more than 1%, and historical attendance at shareholder meetings has never exceeded 30% of total voting rights.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Despite holding LESS than 50%, the investor's 45% stake is likely SUFFICIENT in practice to direct the relevant activities, given the extreme DISPERSION of the remaining shares and historically low voting turnout — this is a classic "DE FACTO CONTROL" scenario requiring consolidation, even without a legal majority.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Exposure to Variable Returns</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">"Returns" is deliberately broad</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Returns can be POSITIVE ONLY, NEGATIVE ONLY, or BOTH — and can include dividends, remuneration for servicing an investee's assets/liabilities, fees, tax benefits, residual interests on liquidation, cost savings, and other benefits NOT available to other interest holders. The key requirement is that returns VARY based on the investee's PERFORMANCE, not that they are guaranteed or fixed.</div>
        <div class="note">Even a SERVICE PROVIDER earning purely fixed, market-rate fees for managing an investee (with no additional variable return) generally does NOT have the requisite "exposure to variable returns" — a critical distinction for asset managers and similar fee-based arrangements (see Section 6's decision maker analysis).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Linkage Between Power and Returns</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Principal vs agent — the decision maker analysis</div>
        <div class="principle">Where an investor holds decision-making rights, it must determine whether it acts as a PRINCIPAL (exercising power for its OWN benefit — controls the investee) or as an AGENT (exercising power on BEHALF of others — does NOT control the investee), considering the scope of its decision-making authority, rights held by other parties, its remuneration, and its exposure to variability of returns from OTHER interests it holds.</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Fund manager — agent, not principal</div>
          <div class="wk-body">An asset management company manages an investment fund, earning a market-based fixed management fee (1% of assets under management) plus a modest performance fee. The fund's other investors hold substantive KICK-OUT RIGHTS, able to remove the manager without cause via a simple majority vote. The manager holds no direct equity interest in the fund.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">The manager is acting as an <strong>AGENT</strong>, not a principal — its remuneration is at market rates commensurate with services provided, its exposure to variable returns is limited, and OTHER investors hold substantive removal rights. The manager does NOT control, and therefore does NOT consolidate, the fund.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Investment Entities Exception</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Fair value through profit or loss, instead of consolidation</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">An entity qualifying as an "INVESTMENT ENTITY" (obtains funds from investors to provide investment management services, commits to an exit strategy, and measures/evaluates investment performance on a fair value basis) does NOT consolidate its subsidiaries — instead, it measures them at FAIR VALUE THROUGH PROFIT OR LOSS, per IFRS 9. This is a DELIBERATE, narrow exception to IFRS 10's general consolidation requirement, reflecting the Framework's relevance principle: for a genuine investment fund, fair value information is MORE relevant to users than a full line-by-line consolidation of underlying portfolio companies.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Loss of Control</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Derecognition and remeasurement at fair value</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">A parent LOSES control when it no longer meets the three control elements (e.g. selling shares, dilution through a new share issue, or losing contractual rights). On loss of control, the parent DERECOGNISES the subsidiary's assets/liabilities and any NCI, RECOGNISES the fair value of consideration received, and recognises any RETAINED interest at its FAIR VALUE at the date control is lost — with the resulting gain/loss recognised in profit or loss.</div>
        <div class="note">The mandatory fair value remeasurement of any RETAINED interest is a genuinely significant recognition event — the retained stake is treated as if NEWLY acquired at fair value on the loss-of-control date, not simply continuing at its previous consolidated carrying amount.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Consolidation Procedures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Uniform accounting policies and full line-by-line combination</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Combine LIKE items of assets, liabilities, equity, income, expenses, and cash flows of the parent with those of its subsidiaries, line by line</span>
          <span>OFFSET the parent's investment in each subsidiary against the parent's portion of equity in each subsidiary (established under IFRS 3 at acquisition)</span>
          <span>ELIMINATE in full intragroup assets/liabilities, equity, income, expenses, and cash flows relating to transactions BETWEEN entities in the group</span>
          <span>Ensure the group applies UNIFORM accounting policies for like transactions, adjusting subsidiary financial statements where necessary</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether de facto control exists absent legal majority voting rights</span>
              <span>Principal vs agent classification for fund managers and similar decision makers</span>
              <span>Whether an entity qualifies for the investment entities exception</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Fair value of a retained interest on loss of control</span>
              <span>Fair value of consideration received or receivable on disposal of a subsidiary</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--chip callout--warn-flush">⚠️ Structured entities and complex financing arrangements often require EXTENSIVE judgement — the control assessment can hinge on subtle contractual details rather than headline ownership percentages.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS vs US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IFRS 10 vs US GAAP</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRS (IFRS 10)</span><span>US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Consolidation model</span><span>SINGLE control model applied to all investee types</span><span>TWO separate models: the VOTING INTEREST model AND a SEPARATE "variable interest entity" (VIE) model for entities lacking sufficient equity or where equity holders lack typical control characteristics</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Structured entities</span><span>Assessed under the SAME single control model as any other investee</span><span>Assessed under the SEPARATE, more complex VIE consolidation model (ASC 810)</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Investment entities</span><span>Fair value exception exists, similar underlying rationale</span><span>Similar investment company accounting exists under ASC 946, broadly converged in spirit</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The MOST significant difference is architectural — IFRS 10 applies ONE unified control model universally, while US GAAP maintains TWO SEPARATE consolidation models (voting interest and VIE), a source of persistent complexity and divergence between the two frameworks.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Line-by-line combination of subsidiary assets/liabilities</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>100% combined, with NCI recognised separately</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Elimination of intragroup balances/transactions</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Full elimination, including unrealised profit on intragroup sales</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>NCI share of subsidiary profit</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge be">Equity</span></span><span>Allocated even if it results in a deficit NCI balance</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Loss of control — derecognition and remeasurement</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Retained interest remeasured to fair value; gain/loss recognised</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Investment entity subsidiaries</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Measured at FVTPL, not consolidated</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Significant judgements, group composition, NCI details</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory disclosure</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IFRS 3</span><span>Applied at the point control is obtained, to recognise assets/liabilities/goodwill at fair value</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IFRS 12</span><span>Supplies detailed disclosure requirements for interests in subsidiaries, joint arrangements, and associates</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 27</span><span>Governs SEPARATE (non-consolidated) financial statements of a parent, complementing IFRS 10's consolidated statement requirements</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IAS 28</span><span>Governs associates and joint ventures NOT meeting IFRS 10's control threshold, using the equity method instead</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IFRS 11</span><span>Governs joint arrangements, distinguished from subsidiaries by the absence of sole control (joint control instead)</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">De facto control</div><div class="glossary-def">Control existing despite holding less than 50% of voting rights, due to the extreme dispersion of remaining shareholders and historical voting patterns.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Kick-out rights</div><div class="glossary-def">Substantive rights held by other parties to remove a decision maker without cause — a key factor in the principal/agent assessment.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Relevant activities</div><div class="glossary-def">Activities of the investee that significantly affect the investee's returns — the activities over which "power" must exist for control to be established.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Control", definition: "Power over an investee, exposure to variable returns, and the ability to use power to affect the amount of those returns — all three present simultaneously." },
      { term: "Power", definition: "Existing rights that give the current ability to direct the relevant activities — the activities that significantly affect the investee's returns." },
      { term: "Relevant activities", definition: "Activities of the investee that significantly affect the investee's returns — the activities over which power must exist for control to be established." },
      { term: "De facto control", definition: "Control existing despite holding less than 50% of voting rights, due to the extreme dispersion of remaining shareholders and historical voting patterns." },
      { term: "Kick-out rights", definition: "Substantive rights held by other parties to remove a decision maker without cause — a key factor in the principal/agent assessment." },
      { term: "Investment entity", definition: "An entity that obtains funds from investors to provide investment management services, commits to an exit strategy, and measures/evaluates investment performance on a fair value basis." }
    ]} />

<FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.IFRS10} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
